_Last updated: 11 September 2026. Every figure on this page was checked against its primary source in September 2026, and I have flagged the numbers that widely repeated "how many websites" roundups cannot actually trace to one._

## How Many Websites Are There in 2026? (Quick Answer)

There are **401.6 million** registered domain names worldwide (Verisign, Q2 2026). Netcraft's crawlers went further and found **1,494,915,628** hostnames responding to a web request in July 2026, spread across **305,348,459** domains. Most of that headline figure is not a working website: Netcraft's own active-site benchmark puts genuinely active sites, ones serving real, distinct content rather than a parked page or a bulk-hosting placeholder, at roughly **216.7 million**, about **15%** of the total.

- **Registered domains:** 401.6 million worldwide, up **8.1%** year on year (Verisign DNIB, Q2 2026)
- **Total hostnames detected:** 1.49 billion, across 305 million domains and 14.8 million web-facing computers (Netcraft, July 2026)
- **Genuinely active sites:** around 216.7 million, roughly 15% of everything Netcraft detects (Netcraft active-site benchmark, July 2026)
- **CMS market share:** WordPress runs **40.3%** of all websites and **58.8%** of sites using a known CMS (W3Techs, September 2026)
- **Top TLD:** .com is used by **43.6%** of all websites; .uk sits at **2.4%** (W3Techs, September 2026)
- **UK specifically:** **8 million** .co.uk domains, the largest single share of the roughly 10.3 million-strong .UK namespace (Nominet, May 2026)
- **Google's index:** roughly **400 billion** documents, the only figure Google has put on record, disclosed in US antitrust trial testimony rather than a public report

> **Want to know how your site compares once you strip out the noise?** A [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) checks whether Google can actually find, crawl and index the pages that matter, rather than counting hostnames the way the headline "billion websites" figures do.

---

Every "how many websites are there" page repeats one of two numbers: 1.1 billion or 1.9 billion, usually without saying where either came from or what it's actually counting. Neither is wrong exactly: they're both reading the same Netcraft survey at different points in time, but neither tells you what you actually want to know, which is how many of those are real, working sites. This page separates the two questions properly: how many domains and hostnames exist, and how many of them are genuinely active. It also covers the UK picture specifically, since most of what I write here is for a UK audience, and UK-focused roundups on this topic are thin on the ground.

## Total Websites and Domains Worldwide in 2026

Two different organisations count two different things, and mixing them up is where most "billions of websites" claims go wrong.

**Verisign** runs the .com and .net registries and publishes the closest thing to an authoritative domain count that exists. Its Domain Name Industry Brief for Q2 2026 reported **401.6 million** domain name registrations across every top-level domain worldwide, up **9.1 million** (2.3%) on the previous quarter and up **29.9 million** (8.1%) year on year. This is a count of registered names, a domain someone has paid a registrar for, whether or not anything is built on it.

**Netcraft** runs a monthly web server survey that instead crawls the live web and counts every hostname that answers an HTTP request. Its July 2026 survey received responses from **1,494,915,628 sites** across **305,348,459 domains** and **14,772,048 web-facing computers**, up 5.5 million sites, 1.2 million domains and 118,277 computers on the month before.

The gap between 401.6 million registered domains and 305 million domains Netcraft actually found responding, and the much larger 1.49 billion "sites" figure, comes down to definitions. Netcraft's "sites" count includes every subdomain and hostname variant that resolves, which is why the sites figure runs roughly five times higher than the domain figure. Neither number tells you how many of those are actually a working website with content on it. For that, you need the active-site figure below.

## Active vs Dormant: How Many Websites Are Actually Live

This is the number that actually matters, and it's the one most roundups skip. Netcraft publishes a smaller active-site benchmark alongside its full survey, intended to represent sites serving distinct, real content rather than parked domains, placeholder pages, redirect-only hostnames, or bulk-registered names nobody has built anything on. As of the July 2026 survey, that active-site figure sits at roughly **216.7 million**, about **14 to 15%** of the 1.49 billion total hostnames Netcraft detects each month.

Put another way: for every seven hostnames the crawlers find, roughly one is a genuinely maintained website. The other six are parked domains sitting on a registrar's default page, placeholder pages from a hosting package nobody finished setting up, redirect-only domains bought to protect a brand, or bulk-registered names bought purely as an asset. Separate research on domain parking backs the same picture from a different angle: CSC's Domain Security Report found roughly a third of third-party-registered domains lead to inactive or broken sites, and closer to half get repurposed purely for pay-per-click advertising rather than genuine content.

This is also why W3Techs, the source behind the CMS figures further down this page, samples a much smaller pool than Netcraft's headline count. W3Techs bases its technology surveys on the top 10 million sites by traffic, deliberately filtering out parked domains and dead hostnames to focus on what it calls "the relevant web," sites with real content and real visitors. If a statistic about website technology or market share doesn't say which pool it's counting from, that's the first thing worth checking.

## How Many New Websites Are Created Each Day?

Here's where I have to be honest rather than repeat a number that sounds precise. Estimates for daily new website creation vary enormously across the sites that publish this figure, from around 252,000 a day (roughly 175 a minute) at the low end to over a million a day at the high end, and none of them link to a named, dated, primary source. That gap exists because **no domain registry or web crawler publishes an authoritative daily gross creation figure**. Verisign reports net registrations at the end of each quarter, Netcraft reports total hostnames detected each month, and neither breaks that down into a verifiable "new sites built today" count. Any specific daily or per-minute figure you see quoted, including the ones on this page in earlier versions, should be treated as an estimate rather than a citable statistic.

What can be verified is the net change. Verisign's Q2 2026 brief shows domain registrations grew by 29.9 million over the preceding twelve months. Spread evenly, that works out to roughly **82,000 net new domain registrations a day** worldwide, across every TLD. This is a net figure, new registrations minus expirations and drops, not a gross count of sites built, and it says nothing about how many of those domains ever get a live website put on them given the active-site data above. It's the only daily-scale number on this page I can trace directly to a primary source, which is exactly why it's smaller than most of the headline claims.

## Which Domain Extensions Dominate? TLD Market Share in 2026

**.com** remains comfortably the dominant top-level domain, used by **43.6%** of all websites W3Techs tracks as of September 2026, roughly four in ten sites on the open web. Verisign's own domain-count data backs this from the registry side too: .com and .net together account for the largest single block of the 401.6 million registrations worldwide.

Country-code domains make up most of what's left. The **.uk** ccTLD (which includes .co.uk, .org.uk and the newer standalone .uk registrations) accounts for **2.4%** of all websites globally, according to W3Techs, making it one of the largest country-code TLDs after Germany's .de. Generic alternatives to .com have not displaced it at any real scale: **.co** on its own sits at just **0.6%** of websites tracked.

| Extension | Share of all websites | Source |
|---|---|---|
| .com | 43.6% | W3Techs, September 2026 |
| .uk (all variants) | 2.4% | W3Techs, September 2026 |
| .co | 0.6% | W3Techs, September 2026 |

For a UK business choosing between .co.uk and the newer standalone .uk, the practical read is the same one I give clients directly: .co.uk still carries the weight of recognition and the vast majority of existing UK registrations, so unless there's a specific brand reason to move, it remains the safer default. The detail behind that is in the UK section below.

## CMS Market Share: How Many Websites Use WordPress?

**WordPress** powers **40.3%** of all websites W3Techs tracks, and **58.8%** of the subset that runs on a content management system it recognises at all (W3Techs, September 2026). That means among sites with an identifiable CMS, roughly three in five run on WordPress, close to eight times the share of its nearest named competitor.

| CMS | Share of all websites | Share of known-CMS websites |
|---|---|---|
| WordPress | 40.3% | 58.8% |
| Shopify | 5.4% | 7.8% |
| Wix | 4.2% | 6.2% |
| Squarespace | 2.4% | 3.5% |

(Source: W3Techs, September 2026)

Worth flagging: **31.5%** of all websites W3Techs surveys use none of the CMS platforms it monitors, meaning custom-built sites, static site generators, or platforms too small to track individually still make up a meaningful chunk of the web. WordPress's dominance is real, but it is dominance of the CMS market specifically, not of every website that exists.

## How Many Websites Does Google Actually Index?

Google has never published a live, official figure for how many pages or sites it has indexed, and the "over 130 trillion pages" line still doing the rounds online traces back to a decade-old blog post about Google's crawl frontier, not its index size. The most concrete number on record came out during the US Department of Justice's antitrust trial against Google, when Pandu Nayak, Google's Vice President of Search, testified under cross-examination that the company's web index held roughly **400 billion documents**. Nayak also acknowledged he could not confirm whether that figure had changed materially since 2020, so treat it as an order-of-magnitude figure rather than a current, precise count.

That works out to roughly 1,846 documents for every active website Netcraft estimates exists, which makes sense once you account for individual pages, not whole sites, being the unit Google actually indexes. A single active website with a few hundred URLs contributes many documents to that 400 billion figure on its own.

## UK Website and Domain Statistics

Since most of the work on this site is for UK businesses, the UK-specific numbers matter more than the global aggregate for most readers here.

**Nominet**, the registry that runs the .UK namespace, reported **8 million** .co.uk domains as of its 30th anniversary update in May 2026, still by far the largest single part of the .UK namespace. The full .UK namespace, covering .co.uk, .org.uk, .me.uk and the standalone second-level .uk registrations introduced in 2014, totals around **10.3 million** domains, with .co.uk holding the largest single share of that total. Nominet reports roughly **168,000** new .UK registrations a month on average through 2026, alongside 7.7 million renewals processed over the past twelve months, a sign the namespace is maturing rather than expanding sharply.

Set against Verisign's 401.6 million global registrations, the UK's 10.3 million .UK-namespace domains represent roughly **2.6%** of every domain registered worldwide, broadly in line with W3Techs' 2.4% website-share figure for .uk overall.

Behind those domain numbers sits a near-universal UK internet population. **94%** of UK adults have home internet access, leaving **6%** offline, according to Ofcom's Adults' Media Use and Attitudes 2026 report, based on fieldwork across 7,533 UK adults. Non-users skew heavily towards the over-75s, a pattern that has held steady for several years running. For a UK business weighing up whether a website is still worth the investment in 2026, the honest answer sits in that 94% figure rather than in any of the more dramatic global "billions of websites" headlines.

## Year-on-Year Growth: Is the Web Still Growing?

Yes, and the clearest verified figure for how fast comes from Verisign's registration data rather than from Netcraft's noisier hostname count. Global domain registrations grew **8.1%** year on year to Q2 2026, adding 29.9 million domains to the 371.7 million registered a year earlier. Growth has not been perfectly steady: quarter-on-quarter growth for Q2 2026 alone came in at 2.3%, roughly a quarter of the annual rate, which points to registrations accelerating rather than compounding evenly across the year.

That 8.1% domain growth rate outpaces UK internet adoption, which is effectively flat given 94% of adults are already online, and it outpaces global population growth many times over. In most developed markets, including the UK, the first-time-adoption phase of the internet is largely finished. Growth instead comes from existing internet users, and the businesses serving them, registering more domains: new brands, new campaigns, new markets, and, per the active-site data above, a fair number of domains that never get built on at all.

## What This Means for Your SEO Strategy

Three things fall out of this data that matter more than the headline "billions of websites" number.

**Competition is smaller than the headline count suggests.** With only around 217 million sites genuinely active out of 1.49 billion hostnames Netcraft detects, the real competitive set for any given search query is a fraction of the number usually quoted. Use that to stop being intimidated by "there are two billion websites" framing and focus instead on the far smaller set of sites actually competing for the same terms and the same audience.

**Most of the web is genuinely dormant, and that's an opportunity for anyone still maintaining a working site.** If roughly 85% of registered hostnames are parked, abandoned, or never built out properly, then a site that is actually maintained, technically sound, and updated on a real schedule already clears a bar most of its nominal "competition" never reaches. A [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) checks exactly the fundamentals, crawlability, indexation, Core Web Vitals, that separate an active, working site from the dormant majority Netcraft counts alongside it.

**The UK's 8 million .co.uk domains are not 8 million active businesses online.** The global active-site ratio is measured on hostnames, not registered domains, so it doesn't translate directly to the UK namespace, but the same directional logic applies: genuinely active UK sites almost certainly number in the low millions, not the headline 10.3 million domain count. Whatever the true UK figure, the practical point holds: most of the sites nominally "out there" in your market are not actively competing for search visibility, which is exactly why the businesses that do invest in ongoing technical and content work tend to separate from the pack quickly. If you want that checked against your own market, I work as a [Reading-based SEO consultant](/services/seo-consultant-reading/) with UK businesses on exactly this kind of technical foundation work.

